[size=4][b]What this is[/b][/size]

Far Cry 2 mods come in two shapes. Most change the game's content: weapon stats, enemy AI, textures, sounds, maps. That lives in data files inside the game's archives, and installing it is a mod manager's job. If that's what you're after, you want the [b]Far Cry 2 Vortex extension[/b]. FCSE is [b]not[/b] a replacement for it.

FCSE covers the rest: the behaviour compiled into the engine itself (bin\Dunia.dll), like how assets are loaded, how the graphics behave, kind of like a meta level of modifications, that no data file can reach. Creating a plugin takes a programmer (or even just a clever Cheat Engine user), but almost anything is possible with it.
FCSE and the Vortex extension work together and touch different files. Moreover, the Vortex extension will even install FCSE plugins for you!

[size=4][b]What it solves[/b][/size]

Until now, changing anything inside Dunia.dll meant shipping a modified copy, or a modified FarCry2.exe. Anything engine level has therefore always been all or nothing.
FCSE is a separate launcher that loads plugins into the game as it starts. Any number of them can change engine behaviour at once, and none of them has to replace a single game file.

[quote][b]Heads up:[/b] FCSE doesn't change anything on its own. It's the foundation other plugins are built on. Install it if a plugin you want lists it as a requirement, or if you want to write one yourself. This is a new framework, so the list of supported plugins is practically empty now.[/quote]

[size=4][b]What you get[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Ability to load plugins. Install as many as you like. They don't fight over game files, because they don't touch any.[/*]
[*]One download for both PC versions. FCSE supports both GOG and Steam versions.[/*]
[*]An in game plugin menu. A real page on the Options screen, named Mod Configuration Manager.[/*]
[*]A log that tells you what happened. [font=Courier New]bin\fcse.log[/font] records every plugin found, everything it changed, and anything that was refused.[/*]
[*]Crash logging. If the game does go down, FCSE writes the exact instruction it died on to [font=Courier New]fcse.log[/font], so you can send it to me if you suspect that there's a bug in FCSE or in one of the plugins.[/*]
[/list]

[b][size=4]➡️ Installation[/size][/b]

[list=1]
[*]Open your Far Cry 2 folder and go into [font=Courier New]bin\[/font], the one with [font=Courier New]FarCry2.exe[/font] and [font=Courier New]Dunia.dll[/font] in it.[/*]
[*]Copy [font=Courier New]FCSE.exe[/font] in there. Don't delete or overwrite anything. [font=Courier New]FCSE.exe[/font] sits [i]next to[/i] [font=Courier New]FarCry2.exe[/font]. It's an extra way to start the game, not a replacement.[/*]
[*]Put your plugins in b[font=Courier New]in\plugins\[/font]. Both .lua scripts and .dll plugins go in that same folder, which is created for you the first time you run FCSE.[/*]
[*]Launch [font=Courier New]FCSE.exe[/font] instead of [font=Courier New]FarCry2.exe[/font].[/*]
[/list]
To confirm it worked: start the game, the splash screen is black and white and if you go to Options, you should see a row for FCSE's plugin menu. bin\fcse.log lists everything that loaded.

To [b]uninstall[/b]: delete [font=Courier New]FCSE.exe[/font].

Steam and other launchers: FCSE is an ordinary executable, so anything that can launch FarCry2.exe can launch FCSE.exe instead. Add it as a non Steam game, repoint your shortcut, add as the default launcher in Vortex, or just run it directly. 

[b][size=4]Compatibility[/size][/b]

Required: Far Cry 2 patch 1.03, in either of the two forms it shipped in on PC:
[list]
[*]Steam / Ubisoft Connect[/*]
[*]GOG, and a retail DVD install patched up to 1.03[/*]
[/list]
[b]Patches 1.00 through 1.02 are not supported. [/b]

[size=4][b]Before you start[/b][/size]

You should only install plugins or scripts from sources you trust.
FCSE itself is open source. Every line of it is on GitHub, linked at the bottom of this page.
No game file is affected by FCSE. Content mods, texture and sound packs, mod managers, dgVoodoo and other wrappers, the map editor. FCSE doesn't touch it.

[line]

[center][b][size=6]For plugin authors[/size][/b]
[i]Everything below is about writing plugins, not installing them.[/i][/center]

[size=4][b]How it works[/b][/size]

FarCry2.exe's [font=Courier New]WinMain[/font] is, in full:

[code]RegisterGameFunctionProvider(&RegisterDebugCommands);
RunGame(hInstance, cmdLine);[/code]

Both are plain exports of Dunia.dll. Nothing meaningful is statically bound into the launcher. FCSE.exe reimplements that entry point, resolves the three exports it needs and inserts a plugin loading step in the middle, before any engine code beyond Dunia's own [font=Courier New]DllMain[/font] and CRT init has run. That's the earliest point at which a plugin can install a hook and be certain nothing it cares about has happened yet.

It also reimplements all 12 of [font=Courier New]RegisterDebugCommands[/font]' handlers byte for byte, so nothing regresses versus the stock launcher. Several of them are live gameplay hooks (diamond pickups, malaria progression, main menu construction), not QA stubs. The two that depend on constants baked into FarCry2.exe's data section are read out of the real executable at startup rather than hardcoded, so the reimplementation is exactly as faithful as whichever build is installed.

Plugins register [i]before[/i] the stock handlers do, and the engine's registry is first claimant wins. That ordering is what lets a plugin take over one of the 12 stock names instead of being locked out of them.

[quote]One difference from the stock launcher that isn't a reimplementation: [b]FCSE.exe is linked [font=Courier New]/LARGEADDRESSAWARE[/font][/b], which FarCry2.exe is not, on either build. The flag lives on the executable and governs the whole process, so starting the game through FCSE gives it the full 4 GB of user address space on 64 bit Windows instead of the 2 GB it is otherwise capped at. This let's you add as much content as possible.[/quote]

[size=4][b]One address, two builds[/b][/size]

Patch 1.03 shipped as two separately compiled builds of the engine on PC. The same function sits at a different address in each, and they are not a fixed distance apart: the offset between them isn't one number, it's [b]thousands of different ones[/b]. No arithmetic turns one address into the other. That is the real reason engine level modding has historically picked one store and ignored the other.

FCSE ships the mapping instead. [b]89,353[/b] functions and globals paired up between the two builds, every one of them with a counterpart on both sides, delta encoded to about 230 KB and embedded in FCSE.exe. You say which build you read an address from, and you get the live address of that same code in whichever build is running:

[code]// C++ - typed, resolved on first use, cached
FCSE::Relocation<void(__thiscall*)(void*)> BeginPageRendering{ FCSE::Uplay (0x005FA9C0) };
FCSE::Relocation<void(__thiscall*)(void*)> SameFunction      { FCSE::Retail(0x005ED140) };[/code]

[code]-- Lua - the same two addresses
local BeginPageRendering = fcse.uplay(0x005FA9C0)   -- read off Steam's Dunia.dll
local same_function      = fcse.retail(0x005ED140)  -- ...or GOG's[/code]

Those two hex numbers name the same function, and either line is correct on either build, so you only ever need the copy of the game you already own. Which build you were looking at is the one thing you have to know about an address, and you always do. An address with no counterpart on the running build comes back as null rather than as a plausible wrong pointer, so a feature can turn itself off instead of jumping somewhere arbitrary. [font=Courier New]Uplay[/font] and [font=Courier New]Retail[/font] are distinct C++ types for that reason too: two [font=Courier New]uint32_t[/font]s that mean entirely different things shouldn't be swappable by accident.

The mapping isn't hand written. It's generated from both DLLs by a pipeline built on Ghidra's own function hashers and on BSim, whose signatures are decompiler derived and therefore survive register reallocation and instruction reordering. Stages run in descending order of certainty, each seeded by what the last one confirmed, and anything that fails a structural check is dropped rather than shipped. It's then checked against two ground truths it wasn't built from: export names, which name the same function on both sides by definition, and string literals, which have to come out identical wherever the data mapping pairs two addresses. The generator, its thresholds and its reports are all in the repository next to what they produced.

[b]Byte patterns[/b] are the other half of the problem. [font=Courier New]FCSE::Pattern("8B 41 04 8B 40 4C C3")[/font], [font=Courier New]api->FindPattern[/font] and Lua's [font=Courier New]fcse.mem.scan[/font] are the same scanner from three directions. Two things it does that a hand rolled one usually doesn't: it searches [font=Courier New].text[/font] only, located from the module's own PE headers rather than by walking the module's file size across mapped memory, and a pattern matching in more than one place returns nothing instead of the first hit, handing back the match count so you can tighten it. Silently taking the first match is how a scan patches the wrong function.

The trade against the address library is a clean one. The table is exact and verified but only knows the two builds it was generated from; a pattern is unverified but works on anything whose code still looks the same, including builds nobody has ever mapped. Trying the table first and falling back to a pattern is a reasonable thing for a plugin to do.

[size=4][b]Two ways to write a plugin[/b][/size]

[b]Lua[/b] is the quickest route and enough for most plugins. A text editor and one file:

[code]local fcse = require 'fcse'

fcse.log('hello from Lua')

fcse.setting{ name = 'Enabled', default = true,
              on_changed = function(value) enabled = value end }

fcse.on('update', function(dt)
  -- runs every frame, dt is the engine's own frame delta in seconds
end)[/code]

Save that as [b]bin\plugins\my_mod.lua[/b] and launch. There is no build step.
Something that needs more than one file can be a folder with a [b]main.lua[/b] inside it ([b]bin\plugins\my_mod\main.lua[/b]); the files beside that main.lua are then libraries to [font=Courier New]require[/font], not scripts that run on their own. Both forms are discovered at any depth under [b]plugins\[/b], so you can group things into folders however you like.

The dialect is [b]Lua 5.1 (LuaJIT)[/b], the same flavour most game modding frameworks use, and not 5.4. It's also unrelated to the ancient Lua 4.1 interpreter compiled into Dunia.dll itself. That one is the engine's own and FCSE doesn't go near it.

[b]C++[/b] is for anything Lua can't reach. The [b]fcse-example-plugin[/b] download is a complete, commented, working plugin: source, header, and a compiled DLL. The ABI is documented inline in [font=Courier New]fcse_api.h[/font], which is the only header a plugin needs and has no dependency on the rest of FCSE's source.

[b]The two example downloads are the same mod twice[/b], once in each language. It's a real one, not a hello world: two toggleable rendering effects, one installed as a hook on an engine method, the other by claiming a named callback, both with a row in the in game menu, both surviving a restart, and both switching off cleanly because neither leaves anything behind to undo. The [b]fcse-example-script[/b] zip extracts straight into [b]bin\[/b]. They use the same seams in the same order, so reading them side by side answers "what does the script version give up?" directly. For that mod, nothing.

The two are deliberately separate downloads, because their prerequisites are. One needs a text editor, the other needs an x86 MSVC toolchain. Scripting exists precisely so that a small change doesn't need a compiler.

[size=4][b]What a script can actually do[/b][/size]

The Lua API is built on LuaJIT's FFI, which keeps the native surface small and composes everything else on top of it:

[list]
[*][b]Memory.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.mem.read_u32/f32/ptr/string/bytes[/font] and their [font=Courier New]write_[/font] counterparts. Reads go straight through FFI. Writes are routed through FCSE's patch manager, which handles [font=Courier New]VirtualProtect[/font], logs the change against your script, and refuses it if another script already claimed those bytes.[/*]
[*][b]Addresses.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.uplay(rva)[/font] and [font=Courier New]fcse.retail(rva)[/font] are the address library described above, and what a script should normally use. [font=Courier New]fcse.base[/font], [font=Courier New]fcse.size[/font], [font=Courier New]fcse.rva(offset)[/font] and [font=Courier New]fcse.to_rva(address)[/font] are the raw arithmetic underneath. Note that [font=Courier New]rva()[/font] is just the load base plus your number, which is the right answer on exactly one of the two builds and unrelated code on the other.[/*]
[*][b]Signature scanning.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.mem.scan('8B 44 24 ?? 85 C0')[/font] and [font=Courier New]scan_all[/font], done natively over Dunia's [font=Courier New].text[/font]. Worth running [font=Courier New]scan_all[/font] first, because a pattern with several hits is not the anchor it looks like. This is the fallback for builds the address library has never seen.[/*]
[*][b]Calling engine functions.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.fn('void(__thiscall*)(void*, int)', fcse.uplay(0x59F210))[/font] gives you a callable. Engine methods are [font=Courier New]__thiscall[/font] and the signature has to say so.[/*]
[*][b]Detouring.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.hook(address, signature, handler)[/font] returns a callable trampoline to the original. MinHook underneath.[/*]
[*][b]Named callbacks.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.command('toRed', handler)[/font] claims one of Dunia's own registry slots by name. No address knowledge at all, and it's a string key, so it doesn't care what build you're on. The cheapest real hook available.[/*]
[*][b]Settings.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.setting{...}[/font] becomes a line in fcse.ini and a row in the in game menu.[/*]
[*][b]Events.[/b] [font=Courier New]load[/font] (before any engine code runs; hook and patch here), [font=Courier New]register_functions[/font] (Dunia's registry exists, and this is the only valid time to call [font=Courier New]fcse.command[/font]), and [font=Courier New]update[/font] (once per frame, from [font=Courier New]CXGame::Update[/font], handed the real frame delta).[/*]
[*][b]Debugging.[/b] [font=Courier New]fcse.log(...)[/font] takes print's argument list, and [font=Courier New]fcse.hex(address, count)[/font] dumps bytes with an ASCII column straight into the log.[/*]
[/list]

A script that throws is named in a traceback, and one that keeps failing is dropped rather than allowed to spam every frame.

[size=4][b]The plugin API, four tiers[/b][/size]

From "no reverse engineering required" to "full control":

[list=1]
[*][font=Courier New]AddFunctionCB(fn, name)[/font] claims a named callback slot. Zero address knowledge, version independent. Around 17 known gameplay call sites are reachable this way.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]Hook(target, detour, &original)[/font] is MinHook backed detouring, for internals with no named seam. [font=Courier New]target[/font] is an address you found yourself, named through [font=Courier New]FCSE::Uplay[/font]/[font=Courier New]FCSE::Retail[/font]/[font=Courier New]FCSE::Pattern[/font] so it stays right on both builds. Exports of any other loaded module work too.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]Patch(address, data, size)[/font] is direct byte patching, applied live and in process. The same kind of edit that used to require shipping a pre patched Dunia.dll, except any number of plugins can now each patch their own ranges.[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]RegisterSettings(name, settings, count)[/font] gives you persistent, player editable settings. Zero address knowledge.[/*]
[/list]

All four are now build independent. Tiers 1 and 4 never needed an address at all; tiers 2 and 3 get theirs from the address library or from a pattern. [font=Courier New]gameBuild[/font], [font=Courier New]gameBuildId[/font] and [font=Courier New]addressMapping[/font] sit on the API struct for the rare plugin that genuinely has to branch on the build, or that wants to log what it ran against. [font=Courier New]duniaSize[/font] is still there and still correct, but it is no longer a version gate: two files of different sizes can be the same engine image.

The header is pure C, so a plugin doesn't have to match the toolchain FCSE was built with, or the one the game was built with. Static CRT is recommended rather than required, and nothing across the boundary transfers ownership.

[size=4][b]Settings and the plugin menu[/b][/size]

A plugin declares what it has (a name, a type, a default and a callback) and FCSE owns the stored value from there:

[code]static const FCSE_Setting settings[] = {
    {"Shake the UI",  FCSE_CHECKBOX(false), &OnShakeChanged, NULL},
    {"Log verbosity", FCSE_CHOICE(1),       &OnChanged, NULL, kVerbosity, 3},
    {"Draw distance", FCSE_SLIDER(5),       &OnChanged, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 10},
    {"Server name",   FCSE_TEXT(),          &OnChanged, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 0, "kilimanjaro", 24},
};
api->RegisterSettings("my_mod", settings, 4);[/code]

Which produces, and thereafter reads back from:

[code][my_mod]
Shake the UI = false
Log verbosity = Normal
Draw distance = 5
Server name = kilimanjaro[/code]

Four types are available to C++ plugins. [b]Checkbox[/b] is a YES/NO spinner, [b]Choice[/b] is a [font=Courier New]< value >[/font] spinner serialized as its label so the file stays readable, [b]Slider[/b] is a draggable range, and [b]Text[/b] opens the game's own modal text prompt. Each renders as the control the game's own settings pages use, so a plugin's page looks like a stock one. The Lua API currently exposes booleans only.

[b]The callback is the only channel.[/b] It fires once from inside registration, synchronously, before any engine code runs, carrying whatever the config file held or your declared default if it held nothing usable. It fires again after every in game toggle. A plugin never needs a separate "read my config" step and never holds a pointer into FCSE's storage.

The page holds [b]20 rows[/b]. That's a hard limit from the engine's own list widget, and FCSE logs an overflow rather than producing rows with no controls under them.

[size=4][b]Conflicts[/b][/size]

Two plugins can legitimately want the same name, address or byte range. Rather than build a hook chaining dispatcher, FCSE tracks per resource ownership and [b]rejects the second claimant[/b], logging both names. Loud and debuggable beats silently wrong.

Which plugin is calling is resolved automatically from the return address, so a plugin never passes its own identity into an API call and therefore can't get it wrong. FCSE's own stock registrations go through exactly the same tracking as anyone else's.

[size=4][b]Logs[/b][/size]

Every run rewrites [b]bin\fcse.log[/b]. Loader lines and plugin lines go through one writer, so the format can't drift between them:

[code][2026-07-27 06:04:03.12341234][fcse] build detection: Far Cry 2 v1.03 (Steam / Ubisoft Connect)
[2026-07-27 06:04:03.12341235][fcse] address library: mapping v1.0, 89353 addresses for fc2_103_uplay
[2026-07-27 06:04:03.12341236][example_plugin] example_plugin loaded[/code]

Timestamps are at Windows' native 100ns resolution, fine enough to order lines from the loader and several plugins landing inside the same millisecond.

The build and the mapping version are in there on every run, so a bug report says which of the two engine builds it came from without anyone having to ask.

A crash handler is installed before anything else runs. It logs the faulting instruction as [font=Courier New]module+RVA[/font], which pastes straight into Ghidra or IDA, along with registers and a scanned return address chain. It's purely an observer: it logs and returns [font=Courier New]EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH[/font], so it doesn't turn crashes into non crashes and doesn't interfere with the game's own handling.

